{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-331276" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],\\,x(t)={e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073694/state.json" "file_sha256" "28c311e21adbdf1de4743a35952dcbb43ec86461e8cdb40b89014b253e4c14bd"} {"question" "q-331276" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & ={e}^{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{59}{4} \\\\ \\frac{11}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{59}{4} \\\\ \\frac{11}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2(\\frac{59}{4})-2(\\frac{11}{2}) \\\\ 2(\\frac{59}{4})-3(\\frac{11}{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{37}{2} \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073694/state.json" "file_sha256" "28c311e21adbdf1de4743a35952dcbb43ec86461e8cdb40b89014b253e4c14bd"} {"question" "q-331276" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073694/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "97993a6d138cc7fb0f3b1debab180f46dc8c61b85ff03ef5aad415f4e300f5f0"} {"question" "q-331276" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073694/state.json" "file_sha256" "28c311e21adbdf1de4743a35952dcbb43ec86461e8cdb40b89014b253e4c14bd"} {"question" "q-331276" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}19 \\\\ \\frac{37}{4}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{29}{2} \\\\ \\frac{11}{2}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{37}{2} \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{59}{4} \\\\ \\frac{11}{2}\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073694/state.json" "file_sha256" "28c311e21adbdf1de4743a35952dcbb43ec86461e8cdb40b89014b253e4c14bd"} {"question" "q-331276" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{37}{2} \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073694/state.json" "file_sha256" "28c311e21adbdf1de4743a35952dcbb43ec86461e8cdb40b89014b253e4c14bd"} {"question" "q-331282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Find equations of the $x$- and $y$-nullclines for the corresponding phase space.$\\,$\nThe $x$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073694/state.json" "file_sha256" "28c311e21adbdf1de4743a35952dcbb43ec86461e8cdb40b89014b253e4c14bd"} {"question" "q-331282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that: \n\n- The $x$-nullcline is a line for which ${x}^{′}=0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are vertical). So, to find it, we set the first equation of the system equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n2x-2y=0\\,⇒\\,y=x\n$$\n\n- The $y$-nullcline is a line for which ${y}^{′}=0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are horizontal). So, to find it, we set the second equation of the system equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n2x+3y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-\\frac{2}{3}x\n$$\n\nPlotting these in the phase space, we obtain the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073694/assets/6ba5066fd4ddc43491c3d007355e0b64c84d449d6462812aa4c8ca0450887a8b.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073694/state.json" "file_sha256" "28c311e21adbdf1de4743a35952dcbb43ec86461e8cdb40b89014b253e4c14bd"} {"question" "q-331282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073694/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "97993a6d138cc7fb0f3b1debab180f46dc8c61b85ff03ef5aad415f4e300f5f0"} {"question" "q-331282" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073694/state.json" "file_sha256" "28c311e21adbdf1de4743a35952dcbb43ec86461e8cdb40b89014b253e4c14bd"} {"question" "q-331282" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3x"] ["math" "-x"] ["math" "2x"] ["math" "x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073694/state.json" "file_sha256" "28c311e21adbdf1de4743a35952dcbb43ec86461e8cdb40b89014b253e4c14bd"} {"question" "q-331282" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073694/state.json" "file_sha256" "28c311e21adbdf1de4743a35952dcbb43ec86461e8cdb40b89014b253e4c14bd"} {"question" "q-331282" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073694/state.json" "file_sha256" "28c311e21adbdf1de4743a35952dcbb43ec86461e8cdb40b89014b253e4c14bd"} {"question" "q-331282" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}x"] ["math" "-\\frac{2}{3}x"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}x"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073694/state.json" "file_sha256" "28c311e21adbdf1de4743a35952dcbb43ec86461e8cdb40b89014b253e4c14bd"} {"question" "q-331282" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{2}{3}x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073694/state.json" "file_sha256" "28c311e21adbdf1de4743a35952dcbb43ec86461e8cdb40b89014b253e4c14bd"} {"question" "q-332627" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x(t)={e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073694/state.json" "file_sha256" "28c311e21adbdf1de4743a35952dcbb43ec86461e8cdb40b89014b253e4c14bd"} {"question" "q-332627" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & ={e}^{2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =9[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1(10)+1(8) \\\\ 1(10)+1(8)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073694/state.json" "file_sha256" "28c311e21adbdf1de4743a35952dcbb43ec86461e8cdb40b89014b253e4c14bd"} {"question" "q-332627" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073694/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "97993a6d138cc7fb0f3b1debab180f46dc8c61b85ff03ef5aad415f4e300f5f0"} {"question" "q-332627" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073694/state.json" "file_sha256" "28c311e21adbdf1de4743a35952dcbb43ec86461e8cdb40b89014b253e4c14bd"} {"question" "q-332627" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073694/state.json" "file_sha256" "28c311e21adbdf1de4743a35952dcbb43ec86461e8cdb40b89014b253e4c14bd"} {"question" "q-332627" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073694/state.json" "file_sha256" "28c311e21adbdf1de4743a35952dcbb43ec86461e8cdb40b89014b253e4c14bd"} {"question" "q-332873" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073694/assets/d9103a8e40d3040b711d0bcfc8cf759d6515ba7d0ae060239d203940e4b09daf.png)\n\nA sketch of the phase space with nullclines and the corresponding direction field of tangents to the solutions of a system of differential equations is shown above (the tangents are not drawn to scale). Which of the following could represent the trajectory starting at the point marked on the graph?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073694/state.json" "file_sha256" "28c311e21adbdf1de4743a35952dcbb43ec86461e8cdb40b89014b253e4c14bd"} {"question" "q-332873" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's sketch the trajectory.\n\n- We start at the given point and go along with the flow (slightly downward and to the right) until the trajectory intersects the $y$-nullcline, where the tangents are horizontal.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073694/assets/146fd1dd846803a8ee5684756a1ddf92b4236b49ad3082bd2397b07eb30671aa.png)\n\n- Next, the flow continues to the right and gradually turns upward.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073694/assets/cf3b24d0c6249d7b60803ea62a06c160b987b2892d4e56f91f0001d276ec4838.png)\n\n- After that, the trajectory keeps going to the right and slightly upward to infinity.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073694/assets/58cefcb6d77335a6dd54aba7bdffdb19024fd8c6aab0659389097ba1ced6d6a0.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073694/state.json" "file_sha256" "28c311e21adbdf1de4743a35952dcbb43ec86461e8cdb40b89014b253e4c14bd"} {"question" "q-332873" "field" nil "attribute" "question/diffi